From a7bf4cb7c8c482cdcd46617a6b1d794beef47fa3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 01:31:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge, windowing/gbm: the end of the Dolby Vision
 signal marked, as a comparison

ETSI GS CCM 001 V1.1.1 gives the header of each HDR DM metadata transmission packet an EOS bit
(clause 6.3.2, table 9, bit 0 of byte 2): "set to indicate that the immediate next picture
contains non-HDR signal. When EOS is equal to 1, HDR sink shall switch to SDR operation mode for
the immediate next picture." Hades sends EOS 0 in every packet and leaves native output with the
restoration alone, in one commit with the mode that Kodi sets next where it can. Whether a
display leaves Dolby Vision better when it is told first (no flash, no blank, sooner back to the
GUI) is not known, and only a display says it.

Now, with <video><dveos>true</dveos></video> in advancedsettings.xml (off by default, as Hades's
other comparisons: dvearlymask, dvaheadalways, dvpasstimes), native output is left with one more
transport frame before the restoration: black, with the packets of the last frame with EOS set in
each and their CRC made again. In CWinSystemGbmGLESContext::EndDVBridge(), after the flip of the
last frame and before the black ordinary frame of the restoration, PresentDVEndOfStream() draws it
into the window as the transport frames are drawn, and commits it as one of them, in a commit that
waits for its flip; the restoration follows as before. Only where the transport is on the output,
not in Dolby Vision Low-Latency (no metadata in the picture), and once for each restoration, not
again when a refused one is tried again (DVBRIDGE::EndOfStreamFrame(), DVEndOfStream.h).

- dvbridge_eos.h (no GL): dvbridge_eos_packets() takes the packets as dvbridge_packets() gives
  them, checks that they are those of a frame (CRC, a first packet that starts a structure, the
  type and version of the header), sets EOS, makes the CRC again (CRC-32 of ISO/IEC 13818-1 annex
  A), and gives the codes of black of the picture that their DM header describes: the YCCtoRGB
  offsets of the first packet (T' = 0), as the source transport takes them, so that the frame is
  black whichever transport the video went out in (256, 2048, 2048 for the converted one).
- dvbridge_gl_pack.c: dvbridge_gl_eos_draw(), the pack of the converted transport with those codes
  in place of a picture: the metadata bits where every packer puts them, the GL state put back.
- CDVBridgeGLES::DrawEndOfStream() takes the packets of the renderer's last frame
  (dvbridge_render_candidate()) and draws it.

kodi.log, without debugging, at each exit where it is on: "DVBridge: native Dolby Vision left with
one more transport frame, black, whose N metadata packets have EOS set (video/dveos, a comparison),
before the output is restored", or "... left without the frame that marks the end of the signal
(video/dveos): <why>; the output is restored as it is without it" (no metadata of the last frame
any more, packets that are not those of a frame, the GPU program, the draw, or the commit
refused).

With the setting off, nothing of the exit changes. Host test dveos_exit_test: EndOfStreamFrame() is
false in every combination with the setting off; EndDVBridge() with the lines that this commit adds
taken out is that of the code before this commit character for character, and FlipDVBridgePage(),
which makes the commits, is unchanged (with the call moved outside its guard, the check fails).

Host test eos_test, on a software OpenGL ES (zink on lavapipe), 43 checks: the CRC's check value;
EOS set in 1 to 5 packets, every other byte as it was and the CRC that checks; black from the
offsets of the converted transport, of other offsets, to the nearest code; packets that are not
those of a frame refused with nothing written; drawn with the converted transport's black and the
packets as they are, the same bytes as dvbridge_gl_pack() of a black picture, flipped and not; with
the EOS packets, every metadata bit read back from the chroma LSBs is that of the EOS packets and
every code black, and the GL state is as it was.

Not run on a display: whether the frame goes out (a commit that the driver accepts after the last
flip), and what a display does with it.
